[{"learnocaml_version":"2","kind":"exercise","title":"Homework 7","short_description":"Suspensions and Infinite Streams","stars":3,"identifier":"hw7"},{"id":"hw7","prelude":"exception NotImplemented\n\ntype 'a susp = Susp of (unit -> 'a)\ntype 'a stream = { head : 'a; tail : 'a stream susp }\n\nlet force (Susp f : 'a susp) = f ()\n\nlet rec take n (s : 'a stream) : 'a list =\n  if n = 0 then [] else s.head :: take (n - 1) (force s.tail)\n\nlet rec zip_with (f : 'a -> 'b -> 'c) (s1 : 'a stream) (s2 : 'b stream) :\n    'c stream =\n  {\n    head = f s1.head s2.head;\n    tail = Susp (fun () -> zip_with f (force s1.tail) (force s2.tail));\n  }\n\nlet rec map (f : 'a -> 'b) (s : 'a stream) : 'b stream =\n  { head = f s.head; tail = Susp (fun () -> (map f) (force s.tail)) }\n\nlet rec iterate (f : 'a -> 'a) (x : 'a) : 'a stream =\n  { head = x; tail = Susp (fun () -> iterate f (f x)) }\n\nlet rec merge (s1 : 'a stream) (s2 : 'a stream) : 'a stream =\n  if s1.head < s2.head then\n    { head = s1.head; tail = Susp (fun () -> merge (force s1.tail) s2) }\n  else if s1.head > s2.head then\n    { head = s2.head; tail = Susp (fun () -> merge s1 (force s2.tail)) }\n  else\n    {\n      head = s1.head;\n      tail = Susp (fun () -> merge (force s1.tail) (force s2.tail));\n    }\n\nlet int_stream_not_implemented : int stream =\n  { head = 0; tail = Susp (fun () -> raise NotImplemented) }\n","template":"(* SECTION 1 *)\n\n(*  Question 1.1 *)\nlet repeat (x : 'a) : 'a stream = raise NotImplemented\n\n(* Question 1.2 *)\nlet rec filter (f : 'a -> bool) (s : 'a stream) : 'a stream =\n  raise NotImplemented\n\n(* Question 1.3 *)\nlet rec lucas1 =\n  {\n    (* You should fix these *)\n    head = 0;\n    tail = Susp (fun () -> raise NotImplemented);\n  }\n\nand lucas2 =\n  {\n    (* You should fix these *)\n    head = 0;\n    tail = Susp (fun () -> raise NotImplemented);\n  }\n\n(* Question 1.4 *)\nlet unfold (f : 'a -> 'b * 'a) (seed : 'a) : 'b stream =\n  raise NotImplemented\n\n(* Question 1.5 *)\nlet unfold_lucas : int stream = int_stream_not_implemented\n\n(* SECTION 2 *)\n\n(* Question 2.1 *)\nlet rec scale (s1 : int stream) (n : int) : int stream = raise NotImplemented\n\n(* Question 2.2 *)\nlet rec s = int_stream_not_implemented\n","descr":[["","<p>In this assignment, we will continue playing with\n<code>stream</code>s. This time, we'll use the record-style definition\nof a stream. We will also use a little wrapper around delayed\ncomputations called <code>susp</code>. Read the prelude, and take note\nof the <code>force</code> function that you can use to call the function\ninside a <code>susp</code>.</p>\n<p>We will <strong>not</strong> be using the caching that was suggested\nat the end of Wednesday's lecture. Doing so is actually a little bit\ntricky in general (for example, if you try using <code>thunk</code> in\n<code>lucas1</code>, you will get a new brand of compile error that you\nprobably haven't seen before!). If you're interested in how this would\nbe done in <em>idiomatic</em> OCaml, check out the <code>Lazy</code>\nmodule.</p>\n<h1 id=\"section-1\">Section 1</h1>\n<h2 id=\"question-11-warm-up\">Question 1.1 (Warm-up):</h2>\n<p>Implement <code>repeat : 'a -&gt; 'a stream</code> that, given an\ninput <code>x</code>, creates a stream <code>s</code> that repeats the\ninput forever.</p>\n<p>You can use the function\n<code>take : int -&gt; 'a stream -&gt; 'a list</code> as seen in class\nto test your code, i.e., <code>take 5 (repeat 2)</code> should return\nyou <code>[2; 2; 2; 2; 2]</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-12-warm-up\">Question 1.2 (Warm-up):</h2>\n<p>Implement\n<code>filter : ('a -&gt; bool) -&gt; 'a stream -&gt; 'a stream</code>\nthat takes a stream <code>s</code> of type <code>'a</code> and a\nfunction <code>f</code> of type <code>'a -&gt; bool</code>, and returns\na filtered stream based on <code>f</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-13\">Question 1.3:</h2>\n<p>You have seen in class how to create a stream for Fibonacci numbers\nusing two streams. To get started with this homework, we ask that you\nimplement the stream of Lucas numbers. It should be quick since it is\nvery close to the Fibonacci sequence.</p>\n<p>You can use the\n<code>zip_with : ('a -&gt; 'b -&gt; 'c) -&gt; 'a stream -&gt; 'b stream -&gt; 'c stream</code>\nfunction we've seen in the lectures, which allows you to combine two\nstreams into one by using the function (or operator) passed in the first\nargument.</p>\n<p>As a reminder, Lucas numbers are defined as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>L(0) = 2</li>\n<li>L(1) = 1</li>\n<li>L(n) = L(n-1) + L(n-2)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We have partially implemented the code for you, your job is to fix\nthe heads and implement the tails of both streams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-14\">Question 1.4:</h2>\n<p>In this question, the goal is to implement\n<code>unfold : ('a -&gt; 'b * 'a) -&gt; 'a -&gt; 'b stream</code>, a\nfunction that will make the generation of streams like Fibonacci or\nLucas numbers much easier. Using\n<code>map : ('a -&gt; 'b) -&gt; 'a stream -&gt; 'b stream</code> which\nyou've seen in class, and the <code>iterate</code> function defined in\nthe prelude which generates a stream by repeatedly applying a function\nto the previous element, implement\n<code>unfold : ('a -&gt; 'b * 'a) -&gt; 'a -&gt; 'b stream</code>.\n<code>unfold f seed</code> constructs a stream by generating elements\nusing <code>f</code> and and initial <code>seed</code>. At each\niteration, <code>f</code> computes the nth value and the seed required\nto get the (n+1)th value. This function is polymorphic on the type of\n<code>seed</code>. E.g.: the stream <code>[x0, x1, x2, ...]</code> is\ngenerated from <code>unfold f seed0</code> as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>[f seed0 = (x0, seed1)]</code></li>\n<li><code>[f seed1 = (x1, seed2)]</code></li>\n<li><code>[f seed2 = (x2, seed3)]</code></li>\n<li>... *</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Your <code>unfold</code> function must not be\nrecursive.</strong></p>\n<p>Hint: generate a sequence of (value, seed) pairs, and then drop the\nseeds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-15\">Question 1.5:</h2>\n<p>Now that we have implemented <code>unfold</code>, use it to generate\na stream of Lucas numbers. You must use <code>unfold</code>.</p>\n<h1 id=\"section-2\">Section 2</h1>\n<p>The goal of this section is to tackle a famous problem first raised\nby Richard Hamming, a well known mathematician. The goal is to\nenumerate, in ascending order and with no repetitions, all positive\nintegers with no prime factors other than 2, 3 or 5. One obvious way to\ndo this is to test each integer, but it is very inefficient since as the\nintegers get larger, fewer of them fit the requirement. In this\nhomework, we will implement another way to solve this problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-21\">Question 2.1:</h2>\n<p>To do so, we will first need a few functions that are going to be\nuseful later.</p>\n<p>Implement <code>scale : int stream -&gt; int -&gt; int stream</code>\nthat multiplies every value of a stream by a constant. For example, if\n<code>ones</code> is an infinite stream of 1s, <code>scale ones 2</code>\nis an infinite stream of 2s. You should use the function\n<code>map : ('a -&gt; 'b) -&gt; 'a stream -&gt; 'b stream</code> given\nin the prelude.</p>\n<p>For the next question, we provide you with a function\n<code>merge : int stream -&gt; int stream -&gt; int stream</code> that\ncombines two ordered streams and produces an ordered stream free of\nduplicates containing the elements from the two streams.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>take 20 (merge pow2 pow3)</code> with\n<code>take 10 pow2 : int list = [2; 4; 8; 16; 32; 64; 128; 256; 512; 1024]</code>\nand\n<code>take 10 pow3 : int list = [3; 9; 27; 81; 243; 729; 2187; 6561; 19683; 59049]</code>\nshould return\n<code>[2; 3; 4; 8; 9; 16; 27; 32; 64; 81; 128; 243; 256; 512; 729; 1024; 2048; 2187; 4096; 6561]</code>.</p>\n<p>You can find <code>merge</code> implemented in the prelude.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-22\">Question 2.2:</h2>\n<p>Now that we have the functions <code>scale</code> and\n<code>merge</code>, we have all the tools we need to answer the main\nquestion of this homework. Reminder: we want to enumerate, in ascending\norder with no repetitions, all positive integers with no prime factors\nother than 2, 3 or 5. In other words, we want to build an infinite\nstream of integers that fit this requirement. Let's name this stream\n<code>s</code>. Note that <code>s</code> has the following\nproperties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>s</code> contains and begins with 1, i.e.,\n<code>s.head = 1</code></li>\n<li>the elements of <code>scale s 2</code>, <code>scale s 3</code>, and\n<code>scale s 5</code> are elements of <code>s</code></li>\n<li>these are all the elements of <code>s</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Using these properties, as well as the <code>scale</code> and\n<code>merge</code> functions, generate a stream that satisfies these\nrequirements.</p>\n"]],"prepare":"pPLGP7SbGLsw9csYKQwgkiVKXkt+C5tS359T","test":"","solution":"","max-score":31,"depend":null,"dependencies":[]},null]